<LessonLayout title="EX3 — Task Manager Agent: File Storage Exercise" num="EX3">

  <LessonHeader
    num="EX3"
    title="Task Manager Agent — File Storage Exercise"
    subtitle="All 10 cookbook phases applied to an agent that reads and writes task files in your project repository — two MCP servers, Roots in practice, file system as persistent state, and the Sampling decision."
  />

  <LessonCallout variant="tip" title="What's new in this exercise vs EX1 and EX2">
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Roots — first actual use.</strong> EX1 (Sales Order Agent) ruled out Roots: HTTP+SSE transport, no filesystem. EX2 (Tech Support) had no filesystem at all. This agent uses stdio + a local filesystem server — Roots is the right tool here.</li>
      <li><strong>Two MCP servers, one agent.</strong> Custom task-manager-server for structured task CRUD + the official filesystem server for repository code access. Two clients, two transports, both scoped by Roots.</li>
      <li><strong>MCP Sampling — a real design option.</strong> The triage step (classifying an incoming bug report) could be implemented server-side via Sampling instead of as an agent node. We examine both options and explain the choice.</li>
      <li><strong>Filesystem as persistent state.</strong> LangGraph state is ephemeral; the files on disk are the source of truth. State holds a session cache, not the canonical record. This creates a class of failure that in-memory state doesn't have.</li>
      <li><strong>Data access pattern applied to task search.</strong> Structured filter vs semantic RAG — both are valid; the framework determines which to use and when.</li>
    </ul>
  </LessonCallout>

  <!-- ── Scenario ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <h2 class="lesson-heading">Scenario — Developer Task Manager</h2>
  <p>
    A TypeScript monorepo project. Tasks live as markdown files with YAML frontmatter
    under <code>.tasks/open/</code> and <code>.tasks/closed/</code> inside the repository.
    The agent is invoked from the terminal:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Interactive:</strong> <code>task-agent "what should I work on today?"</code></li>
    <li><strong>External input:</strong> <code>gh issue view 142 | task-agent --triage</code> — pipe in a GitHub issue and triage it into a task file</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The agent can create, read, update, search, and close tasks; link tasks to source files;
    generate a standup report from recent activity; and reason about what to work on next
    based on priorities and the current git state.
  </p>

  <CodeBlock lang="markdown" label=".tasks/open/T-0042.md (example task file)" code={taskFileSchemaCode} />

  <!-- ── Worked example ────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <h2 class="lesson-heading">Worked Example — Applying the 10 Phases</h2>

  <!-- Phase 01 -->
  <h3 class="lesson-heading">Phase 01 — Do you need an agent?</h3>
  <div class="lesson-table">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Decision question</th><th>Answer</th><th>Verdict</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Fixed, known steps?</td>
          <td>Simple CRUD on task files is fixed — a CLI with <code>task create</code>, <code>task list</code> commands would work. No agent needed for that.</td>
          <td>—</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Does the user's intent require reasoning to determine what to do?</td>
          <td>Yes — "what should I work on today?" requires reasoning about priority, estimated hours, blocked tasks, recent commits, and current focus. A CLI can't answer that question.</td>
          <td>—</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Are inputs unstructured and variable?</td>
          <td>Yes — triage mode accepts raw GitHub issues, Slack messages, bug reports. The agent must extract structured fields from freeform text. A regex or template can't handle the variance.</td>
          <td>—</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Dynamic tool selection based on query?</td>
          <td>Yes — "anything blocking the auth refactor?" might need search_tasks + get_task + read source files. Which tools to call, in what order, depends on what each returns.</td>
          <td><strong>Agent justified.</strong></td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <!-- Phase 02 -->
  <h3 class="lesson-heading">Phase 02 — Define the goal and the problem</h3>
  <div class="phase-def">
    <div class="pd-row"><span class="pd-label">Goal</span><span class="pd-val">Give a developer instant situational awareness of their project backlog and reduce friction in capturing, organising, and acting on tasks — all without leaving the terminal.</span></div>
    <div class="pd-row"><span class="pd-label">Success metric</span><span class="pd-val">Task creation time &lt; 30 s · Zero orphaned tasks (every task is either closed or assigned) · Standup report generation &lt; 5 s · External task triage accuracy ≥ 90% (correct priority and tags) confirmed by dev</span></div>
    <div class="pd-row"><span class="pd-label">Input</span><span class="pd-val">Natural language query (interactive) <em>or</em> unstructured text piped from stdin (triage mode)</span></div>
    <div class="pd-row"><span class="pd-label">Output</span><span class="pd-val">Answer / task action performed / report generated / new task file written to <code>.tasks/open/</code></span></div>
    <div class="pd-row"><span class="pd-label">"Done" means</span><span class="pd-val">User's question answered, or task state on disk changed as intended and confirmed, or report printed to stdout.</span></div>
    <div class="pd-row"><span class="pd-label">Hard constraints</span><span class="pd-val">Never close a task without explicit user confirmation · Never read files outside the project root (enforced by Roots) · Never create duplicate tasks for the same issue</span></div>
    <div class="pd-row"><span class="pd-label">Acceptable failure</span><span class="pd-val">"I couldn't find any tasks matching that." Do NOT hallucinate a task that doesn't exist on disk.</span></div>
  </div>

  <!-- Phase 03 -->
  <h3 class="lesson-heading">Phase 03 — Choose your autonomy level: autonomy per action</h3>
  <div class="lesson-table">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Action</th><th>Autonomy level</th><th>Reason</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>list_tasks</code>, <code>get_task</code>, <code>search_tasks</code></td><td>Fully automatic</td><td>Read-only — no disk change, no side effect</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>update_task</code> — status, priority, tags, linked files</td><td>Automatic</td><td>Low risk; files are version-controlled — git can recover any update</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>create_task</code></td><td>Automatic with preview</td><td>Show the task title and priority before writing. User should confirm they want a new file, not an update to an existing one.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>close_task</code></td><td><strong>Human confirmation required</strong></td><td>Moves file from <code>open/</code> to <code>closed/</code>. While version-controlled, recovering from an accidental close requires a git operation the user may not know to run. Always confirm.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Reading source code files</td><td>Automatic when user asks</td><td>Read-only; scoped to project root by Roots — agent cannot escape the project directory</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Triage enrichment (priority, tags from external input)</td><td>Suggest + user confirms before write</td><td>Triage is a suggestion; the developer must agree to the priority before the task is written to disk</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <!-- Phase 04 -->
  <h3 class="lesson-heading">Phase 04 — Design the tools: tool inventory</h3>
  <div class="lesson-table">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Tool</th><th>Server</th><th>Type</th><th>Reversible?</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>list_tasks(status?, priority?, tags?, assignee?)</code></td><td>task-manager-server</td><td>Read · structured filter</td><td>—</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>get_task(task_id)</code></td><td>task-manager-server</td><td>Read · key lookup</td><td>—</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>create_task(title, description, priority, tags, ...)</code></td><td>task-manager-server</td><td>Write</td><td>Yes (git)</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>update_task(task_id, changes)</code></td><td>task-manager-server</td><td>Write</td><td>Yes (git)</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>close_task(task_id, resolution)</code></td><td>task-manager-server</td><td>Write · destructive</td><td>Recoverable via git only</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>search_tasks(query, status?)</code></td><td>task-manager-server</td><td>Read · full-text</td><td>—</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>read_file(path)</code></td><td>official filesystem server</td><td>Read · key lookup</td><td>—</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>list_directory(path)</code></td><td>official filesystem server</td><td>Read · key lookup</td><td>—</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <LessonCallout variant="tip" title="Data access pattern for task search — structured filter vs RAG">
    <p>
      Two different search needs, two different patterns. Apply the data access framework (CB Phase 04 / L17):
    </p>
    <div class="lesson-table">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Query type</th><th>Example</th><th>Pattern</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Structured filter</td>
            <td>"Show all high-priority auth tasks"</td>
            <td><strong><code>list_tasks</code></strong> (filter on frontmatter)</td>
            <td>Priority and tags are structured fields in YAML frontmatter. A filter over parsed frontmatter is instant, exact, and needs no embedding.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Semantic query</td>
            <td>"Anything related to the login flow?"</td>
            <td><strong><code>search_tasks</code></strong> (full-text / RAG)</td>
            <td>"Login flow" won't appear in the tag list; it requires matching against task titles and descriptions.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      For <code>search_tasks</code>: with a small task list (&lt; 200 tasks), simple full-text search (grep over
      markdown bodies) is sufficient and avoids a vector index. If the backlog grows to thousands of tasks,
      switch to embedding task descriptions into a local vector store. The tool interface doesn't change —
      only the implementation under the hood.
    </p>
    <p>
      The key principle: <strong>don't embed what you can filter</strong>. Frontmatter exists precisely to
      avoid semantic search for structured attributes.
    </p>
  </LessonCallout>

  <h3 class="lesson-heading">Phase 04 — Design the tools: two MCP servers</h3>
  <p>
    The 8 tools come from two servers for a deliberate reason: the task-manager-server understands
    the <code>.tasks/</code> schema and validates frontmatter on every write. The official filesystem
    server is generic — it reads any file. Mixing schema-aware writes with generic filesystem reads
    in one server would conflate two responsibilities.
  </p>
  <div class="lesson-table">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Server</th><th>Transport</th><th>What it provides</th><th>Why separate</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>task-manager-server</strong> (custom)</td>
          <td>stdio</td>
          <td>list_tasks, get_task, create_task, update_task, close_task, search_tasks + task file Resources</td>
          <td>Schema validation, ID generation, and file organisation logic live here. The agent gets structured task objects, not raw markdown.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>@modelcontextprotocol/server-filesystem</strong> (official)</td>
          <td>stdio</td>
          <td>read_file, list_directory — scoped to project root</td>
          <td>The agent needs to read source files when a user says "look at src/auth/middleware.ts and tell me what the task is about." The official server provides this without custom code.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    Both servers use stdio transport and both operate on the local filesystem — Roots is applicable
    to both. The client declares the project root as a Root for each server independently.
  </p>

  <CodeBlock lang="typescript" label="mcp-client-setup.ts" code={mcpClientCode} />

  <CodeBlock lang="typescript" label="task-manager-server.ts" code={taskMcpServerCode} />

  <LessonCallout variant="key" title="Roots in practice — why it matters here but not in EX1">
    <p>
      The Roots capability scopes a filesystem MCP server to specific directories. It applies when
      <strong>two conditions are both true</strong>: stdio transport (local process) AND the server reads/writes
      the filesystem.
    </p>
    <p>
      In EX1 (Sales Order Agent): the <code>sales-tools-server</code> ran over HTTP+SSE and called APIs
      and databases — it never touched the filesystem. Roots was irrelevant.
    </p>
    <p>
      Here: both servers run as local child processes via stdio and both access the project directory.
      Without Roots, <code>server-filesystem</code> would have access to the entire machine — the user's
      home directory, credentials, SSH keys. Declaring the project root as a Root scopes it to exactly
      the files the agent should be able to see.
    </p>
    <p>
      Roots is not an optional courtesy — it is the security boundary for local filesystem servers.
    </p>
  </LessonCallout>

  <!-- Phase 04 — MCP Sampling decision -->
  <h3 class="lesson-heading">Phase 04 — Design the tools: the Sampling decision</h3>
  <p>
    The triage step (classifying an incoming bug report into priority + tags) could be implemented
    in two ways. This is one of the few places in the cookbook where MCP Sampling is a genuine
    design option, not just a theoretical possibility.
  </p>
  <div class="lesson-table">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Option</th><th>How it works</th><th>Trade-offs</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>A — Agent-side triage node</strong> (chosen)</td>
          <td>Triage node (Haiku) runs in the LangGraph graph. The server receives a completed <code>create_task</code> call with priority and tags already set.</td>
          <td>Agent controls the model, cost, and prompt. Observable in LangSmith. Can be tested independently. Server stays simple — it only stores what it receives.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>B — MCP Sampling</strong></td>
          <td>Server receives raw text. Server calls <code>client.createMessage()</code> to ask the client's LLM to classify it. Server writes the enriched task to disk using the classification result.</td>
          <td>Server becomes more autonomous. But: server now depends on client LLM capability, adding coupling. Classification errors are harder to trace (happen inside the server process). Server has no LLM fallback if the client doesn't support Sampling.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <LessonCallout variant="tip" title="When MCP Sampling makes sense">
    <p>
      MCP Sampling fits when the server has information the agent can't efficiently retrieve and
      needs an LLM to interpret it — for example, a server reading a large codebase that wants to
      summarise a file before returning it. The LLM call happens close to the data, avoiding a
      round-trip of raw content back to the agent.
    </p>
    <p>
      It's the wrong tool when the classification is a core agent concern (the agent should see the
      result and decide what to do with it), or when you need the call to be testable, observable,
      and independently tuneable. Both are true for triage — the agent should review the extracted
      priority before writing it to disk. Agent-side is correct here.
    </p>
    <p>
      To use Sampling, declare it in the client capabilities and handle the server's
      <code>sampling/createMessage</code> request. We deliberately do <em>not</em> declare it
      in this agent, which means the server cannot call it — the capability is simply absent.
    </p>
  </LessonCallout>

  <!-- Phase 05 -->
  <h3 class="lesson-heading">Phase 05 — Design state &amp; memory: filesystem as source of truth</h3>
  <p>
    This agent has an unusual state design: <strong>the filesystem IS the persistent store</strong>.
    LangGraph state is a session cache — it holds task frontmatter loaded at the start of each
    interaction, not a canonical database. Everything written to state is derived from disk.
    Everything written to disk via the MCP server persists beyond the session.
  </p>
  <p>
    This creates a cache invalidation problem: if a teammate edits a task file directly (not via
    the agent), the in-session cache is stale. The strategy: load <code>taskCache</code> fresh at
    the start of each interaction using <code>list_tasks</code>, and call <code>get_task</code>
    on demand for the full body. Never assume the cache is current mid-session without re-loading.
  </p>

  <CodeBlock lang="typescript" label="task-manager-state.ts" code={stateCode} />

  <LessonCallout variant="warn" title="In-session cache is not the source of truth">
    <p>
      The <code>taskCache</code> field holds frontmatter loaded at interaction start.
      It is valid for the duration of one interaction. It is <strong>not</strong> a database.
    </p>
    <p>
      If the agent updates a task during the session (<code>update_task</code>), it must also update
      the cached frontmatter in state — otherwise subsequent list queries in the same session return
      stale data. The MCP server's <code>listChanged: true</code> Resource capability sends a
      notification when files change on disk, which the agent can use to invalidate the cache.
    </p>
  </LessonCallout>

  <!-- Phase 06 -->
  <h3 class="lesson-heading">Phase 06 — Single agent or multi-agent?</h3>
  <div class="lesson-table">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Criterion</th><th>This system</th><th>Split?</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Tool count</td><td>6 tools (task-manager-server) + 2 from filesystem server = 8 total. Above guideline.</td><td>—</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Conflicting responsibilities in one system prompt?</td><td>Yes — triage (extract fields from freeform text, no filesystem) and task management (CRUD, search, reporting) are different tasks. Triage benefits from Haiku; task management benefits from Sonnet with a richer prompt.</td><td><strong>Yes — split Triage out</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Different models/tools per path?</td><td>Triage: Haiku, no tools, returns JSON. Task Manager: Sonnet, 6-8 tools, conversational. Different models justified.</td><td><strong>Yes — two nodes</strong></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="lesson-table">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Component</th><th>Agent?</th><th>Responsibility</th><th>Model / tools</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Context loader</strong></td>
          <td>No — pure function</td>
          <td>Resolves project root, loads open task frontmatter into <code>state.taskCache</code> via <code>list_tasks</code>.</td>
          <td>No LLM · <code>list_tasks</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Triage node</strong> <em>(external-task mode only)</em></td>
          <td>Yes — LLM agent</td>
          <td>Reads <code>state.externalInput</code>. Extracts title, description, priority, tags as structured JSON. Fires only when <code>state.inputMode === 'external-task'</code>.</td>
          <td>Haiku · No tools</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Task Manager</strong></td>
          <td>Yes — LLM agent</td>
          <td>Handles all user queries, task CRUD, search, report generation, code file reading. After triage: presents the extracted fields, asks user to confirm, then calls <code>create_task</code>.</td>
          <td>Sonnet · <code>list_tasks</code>, <code>get_task</code>, <code>create_task</code>, <code>update_task</code>, <code>close_task</code>, <code>search_tasks</code>, <code>read_file</code>, <code>list_directory</code></td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>3 nodes. 2 are LLM agents. The Task Manager has 8 tools — above the 3–5 guideline — but splitting
  it further would be artificial: all 8 tools serve a single coherent responsibility (manage project tasks
  and provide code context). The tool descriptions are highly distinct; there is no ambiguity about when
  to call which one.</p>

