  <!-- Phase 08 -->
  <h3 class="lesson-heading">Phase 08 — Add safety &amp; guardrails</h3>
  <div class="lesson-table">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Layer</th><th>What it catches</th><th>Applied to</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Roots enforcement</strong></td>
          <td>Filesystem server cannot access paths outside the declared project root. If the agent tries to read <code>/home/user/.ssh/id_rsa</code>, the server rejects it.</td>
          <td>Both MCP servers, enforced at connection time</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Schema validation on write</strong></td>
          <td>task-manager-server validates frontmatter against the Zod schema before any file write. Malformed data (wrong priority enum, missing title) is rejected before touching disk.</td>
          <td>create_task, update_task, close_task</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>close_task confirmation</strong></td>
          <td><code>interrupt()</code> before any <code>close_task</code> call. System prompt and the MCP tool description both state this requirement.</td>
          <td>close_task tool call</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Duplicate detection</strong></td>
          <td>Before <code>create_task</code>, the Task Manager calls <code>search_tasks(title)</code> to check for an existing task with a similar title. Surfaces matches and asks the user whether to create a new task or update the existing one.</td>
          <td>create_task flow</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Injection via external input</strong></td>
          <td>Piped input in triage mode could contain adversarial instructions. The Triage node is given only the raw text — it does not receive the system prompt for the Task Manager. Triage returns JSON; the Task Manager acts on the JSON, not the raw input.</td>
          <td>Triage node</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <!-- Phase 09 -->
  <h3 class="lesson-heading">Phase 09 — Error handling &amp; resilience: filesystem-specific failures</h3>
  <div class="lesson-table">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Failure</th><th>Response</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Task file modified by teammate during session (stale cache)</td>
          <td>Before any write operation, re-fetch the task via <code>get_task(task_id)</code> to confirm current state. If it changed, show the diff and ask the user to confirm the write still applies.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>create_task</code> fails because <code>.tasks/open/</code> doesn't exist yet</td>
          <td>task-manager-server creates the directory on first write. The agent never needs to handle this — it's a server-level concern.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Frontmatter YAML is malformed in an existing task file</td>
          <td><code>get_task</code> returns a parse error. Agent reports: "T-0042 has a malformed frontmatter — cannot read it. The file is at .tasks/open/T-0042.md if you want to fix it manually."</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Official filesystem server not installed</td>
          <td>Agent starts without the filesystem client. All task operations work; only <code>read_file</code> and <code>list_directory</code> are unavailable. Agent tells user: "Source file reading is unavailable — run <code>npm install @modelcontextprotocol/server-filesystem</code>."</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Agent asked about a task ID that doesn't exist</td>
          <td>"No task found with ID T-9999." Do NOT hallucinate a task description. Do NOT search for "something similar" unless the user asks.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Triage extracts priority=critical for a trivial request</td>
          <td>Triage result is always shown to the user for confirmation before writing. The user changes the priority before the task is created. Triage is a suggestion, not a decision.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

